<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis</h1><p class="st-meta">41 CE – 92 CE (approx.) · capital Chalcis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Roman client kingdom in the Beqaa Valley given to members of the Herodian family, whose kings also controlled the Jerusalem high priesthood.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>, had been the seat of an <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ituraea">Ituraean principality</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-son-of-mennaeus">Ptolemy son of Mennaeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysanias-of-chalcis">Lysanias</a>. In 41 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> gave it with the title king to Herod, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa I</a>, now known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a> also received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> authority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> and the right to appoint high priests, an influence passed on to his successors. After his death in 48, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> went to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Agrippa II</a>, who exchanged it in 53 for larger lands further south. Little is known of internal government; the population was mostly Ituraean Arab and Syrian, with Greek used in administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 57 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-of-chalcis">Aristobulus</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a> and husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salome-daughter-of-herodias">Salome</a>, who also held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lesser-armenia">Lesser Armenia</a>. After his reign the territory was annexed to the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> around 92.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis.jpg" alt="Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Eusebius · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anjar_-_Cardo_vu_du_nord_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>41 CE – 92 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalcis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herod of Chalcis, Agrippa II, Aristobulus of Chalcis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greco-Roman polytheism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chalcis ad Libanum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=66&amp;focus=herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis" aria-label="Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5597 955 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5917 1211L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5917 1211h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5917 1211h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=66&amp;focus=herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
